WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.731
FedRAMP is built on NIST guidance, particularly NIST Special Publication 800-53.

00:00:04.731 --> 00:00:09.783
NIST SP 800-53 provides a catalog of security and privacy controls used

00:00:09.783 --> 00:00:12.309
across federal information systems and programs.

00:00:12.309 --> 00:00:13.150
In FedRAMP,

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:18.202
those controls provide the foundation for many security requirements and assessment activities.

00:00:18.202 --> 00:00:23.675
They help define what a cloud service provider needs to implement, document, assess,

00:00:23.675 --> 00:00:24.517
and monitor.

00:00:24.517 --> 00:00:28.306
Controls cover many areas, including access control, incident response,

00:00:28.306 --> 00:00:31.253
configuration management, contingency planning, identification and authentication,

00:00:31.253 --> 00:00:35.042
audit logging, risk assessment, system communications, and vulnerability management.

00:00:35.042 --> 00:00:41.777
The NIST relationship is important because FedRAMP is not invented separately from federal risk management practices.

00:00:41.777 --> 00:00:47.671
It is connected to the same control language used in broader federal cybersecurity governance.

00:00:47.671 --> 00:00:52.302
Cloud providers and agencies use these controls to structure security plans,

00:00:52.302 --> 00:00:55.249
implementation statements, assessment procedures, findings, remediation plans,

00:00:55.249 --> 00:00:56.512
and monitoring activities.

00:00:56.512 --> 00:00:59.880
Control implementation is not just a paperwork activity.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:04.090
A control may involve technical configuration, operational procedures, policy, monitoring,

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:06.194
evidence generation, and clear ownership.

00:01:06.194 --> 00:01:07.036
For learners,

00:01:07.036 --> 00:01:12.088
the key takeaway is that FedRAMP translates NIST control expectations into a

00:01:12.088 --> 00:01:16.298
standardized cloud security assessment and monitoring model for federal use.

00:01:16.298 --> 00:01:18.824
This connection also helps technical teams.

00:01:18.824 --> 00:01:22.613
When engineers understand the control families behind FedRAMP requirements,

00:01:22.613 --> 00:01:25.560
they can connect architecture, configuration, logging, identity,

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.507
and operational practices to specific risk objectives.

00:01:28.507 --> 00:01:32.716
The control language provides a bridge between implementation and governance.

00:01:32.716 --> 00:01:37.768
It lets a technical safeguard become part of a larger story about

00:01:37.768 --> 00:01:42.820
how the cloud service protects federal information and supports agency risk decisions.
